{n}She does not untie it. She puts you against the edge of the table instead, among the wax and the charcoal, and keeps one hand on the ribbon at the back of your head, where the hook is. The painted smile is cold against your cheek when she turns your face to the candlelight; her other hand drags your shirt open and her mouth follows it, hot where the mask is cold.{/n}
"Pull and you leave your hair on my table," {n}she breathes.{/n} "Istrava gives her quarry a head start. I give you none. Keep very still, quarry, and let me hunt."
{n}Her breath is hot beneath the cold paint, and the lodge, the stairs and the candles go out of your mind.{/n}
